rongly disfavors B4 (neurogenic dysfunction) despite the weak pulses, which initially suggested B4.", "Lack of heart failure signs weakly disfavors B6 (cardiac-related GI symptoms). This reduces support for B6 but does not eliminate it."], "evidence_against": ["The patient's history of constipation provides weak support for B2. However, absence of a palpable fecal mass (LR-≈0.3) and subacute symptoms reduce its plausibility.", "The surgical scar and distension moderately favor B3 (structural obstruction) but are counterbalanced by the absence of abdominal tenderness/cramping (LR-≈0.4), leaving B3 at moderate_for.", "Weak pulses moderately support B4 (neurogenic dysfunction) but are contradicted by absence of fecal incontinence (LR-≈0.2) and subacute symptoms, which weakly disfavor B4.", "The murmur and hypertension strongly support B6 (cardiac-related GI symptoms) but are tempered by the absence of heart failure signs (LR-≈0.9882) and subacute course, which weakly disfavor B6.", "Subacute duration and lack of fever/discharge weakly disfavor B1.1 (norovirus), shifting support toward B4 and B6 as non-infectious explanations."], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Has the patient had recent travel or exposure to sick individuals?", "Are there signs of systemic infection like fever or leukocytosis?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Stool culture", "Serology for norovirus"]}, "target_level": 2}
